#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""[" -f "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" ] || { echo "a Python script: run it with python3" >&2; exit 126; }; exec python3 "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" "$@" || exit # sh/bash: hand off to python3 before reading the docstring
Is this skill installed, and is it current?

Prints ONE line and exits:

    0  installed, current      -> proceed
    1  installed, but stale    -> re-run INSTRUCTION.md, then re-check
    2  not installed           -> follow INSTRUCTION.md

State 1 is the reason this exists. Absent is loud: the watcher exits with
WATCH-CONFIG and nothing arrives. Stale is silent: everything imports,
everything runs, and the behaviour is subtly the old one.

THE INSTALL'S FILES ARE READ FROM THE INSTALL CONTEXT, never from beside this
script: config.json and installation/.install-state.json are SPECIFIC files, and
this skill directory holds the mechanism only. fleetlib.paths.skill_context()
names the directory (`fleet where slack-communication` prints the same one), so
the check reads exactly the files the install's agents read.

.install-state.json is a SPECIFIC file: it is committed like everything else,
so it travels to machines that never ran the install. That is why it records the
host it was written on and why this script checks it: without that, a pull would
make every other machine read as "installed and current".

It verifies REAL ARTIFACTS, never a marker file. The strongest artifact test
available is the script's own loader: this imports watch.py with
SLACK_WATCH_CONFIG set to the install's config.json, so the config is parsed,
every required key is resolved and the token path is resolved by the same code
the watcher uses. A marker file can lie; that cannot.

⚠️ AND IT VERIFIES THEM BY PERFORMING THE OPERATION THE WATCHER PERFORMS, NEVER
BY ASKING WHETHER THEY EXIST. exists() and getsize() are stat(2): they answer
from the inode and never open anything, so a token file that is present, is the
right size and CANNOT BE READ passes both and the check reports the install
green. Measured 2026-09-09 on this machine, on a mode-000 copy of a real token:
os.path.exists -> True, os.path.getsize -> 59, open(...).read(1) -> EACCES. That
is the whole failure class: every one of these paths is consumed by opening it,
so opening it is the only test whose green means anything. read_first_byte below
is that test, and REPLACING ANY CALL TO IT WITH exists()/getsize() SILENTLY
RESTORES THE BLIND SPOT - the check keeps passing on a machine where the watcher
cannot start, which is the one outcome it exists to prevent. Do not.

⚠️ AND OPENING IS NOT FREE OF ITS OWN HAZARD, WHICH IS WHY read_first_byte USES
O_NONBLOCK AND CHECKS S_ISREG. A blocking open on a FIFO waits for a writer
forever; measured 2026-09-09, a FIFO at token.path takes a stat-based check to
"empty" in milliseconds and a plain open(path,"rb") has to be killed. Read that
function's docstring before simplifying it to a plain `open()`.

⚠️ THE NEGATIVE CONTROL FOR THAT CLAIM, AND WHAT IT COSTS TO SKIP IT. Point the
variable token.path in config.json uses at a copy of the token store - for a
token kept in the install's organisation store that is FLEET_SECRETS_DIR, whose
fallback is the legacy variable the install manifest names in
secrets.legacy_env - `chmod 000` the token file there, and re-run: this must
exit 2 naming the token file and the chmod that fixes it. ⚠️ IF THAT CONTROL
PASSES, ISSUE NO VERDICT ABOUT THIS INSTALL - a check that goes green on an
unreadable token is not reading it, and its green is decoration. There are three
outcomes here and only two of them are a verdict: the control red (the check
works), the control green (the check is broken - say INCONCLUSIVE, not OK), and
the control never reaching the token leg at all because an earlier leg exited
first (also INCONCLUSIVE - read the printed line to tell which leg spoke).

⚠️ EVERY FAILURE NAMES A COMMAND A HUMAN RUNS. A permission or ownership problem
on a file under a secrets store is not an agent's to repair - an agent that could
chmod the token store could also read every other credential in it - so the line
hands the operator the exact `chmod`/`chown` rather than describing the state and
leaving them to derive one.

THE CLAIMS DIRECTORY LEG (claims_dir_problem). chat-transport's claim.py decides
which ONE agent acts on a request by hard-linking a claim file into one
host-wide directory - host.json's claims_dir - and it never creates that
directory. This leg is the only thing that says whether the directory is fit for
that, and each property it requires is there because its absence is silent at
claim time:
  - RESOLVED THROUGH claim.configured_dir(), never by parsing host.json here:
    a second parser is a second answer to "which directory is the fleet's",
    and the two would drift toward a check that is green about a directory the
    script does not use.
  - lstat, and NOT A SYMLINK: a link can be repointed under running agents, and
    the agents that resolved it before and after then claim in two directories,
    where two of them win the same request.
  - OWNED BY THE RUNNING USER, MODE 700: every agent runs as that user; a group-
    or world-writable directory lets any other local account create a claim
    file for every request, after which every agent stays out silently and
    nobody answers anything.
  - WRITABLE, PROVEN BY PERFORMING claim.py's OWN OPERATION: create a file with
    O_EXCL, fsync it, link(2) it to a second name, and require a second link
    to the same name to be REFUSED - the exactly-one-winner guarantee rests on
    that refusal, and a filesystem without hard links would fail every take
    with exit 3. os.access() and the mode bits answer from the inode; they are
    green on a read-only mount and on a filesystem that has no link(2).
⚠️ ITS NEGATIVE CONTROLS, and what to conclude if one passes. Point
FLEET_HOST_CONFIG at a copy of host.json whose claims_dir is a path you own, and
run the check for each of: a path that does not exist (must exit 2 naming
the `fleet install` that creates it), a symlink to a mode-700 directory,
a regular file, and a mode-755 directory (each exit 2 naming the fix), and a
real mode-700 directory (must exit 0). IF ANY BAD CASE EXITS 0, ISSUE NO
VERDICT ABOUT THE INSTALL: this leg is not measuring the directory. A leftover
`.install-check.*` file in the directory means a check was killed mid-probe;
nothing reads it, and it is safe to delete.
"""
import errno
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import secrets
import socket
import stat
import sys

# realpath: through a personal skill link `abspath` names the agent's config
# directory, whose parents are not the fleet tree.
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
SKILL = os.path.dirname(HERE)
FLEET_ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(SKILL)))
SKILL_NAME = os.path.basename(SKILL)


def out(code: int, msg: str) -> None:
    print(msg)
    sys.exit(code)


def declared_version() -> int:
    """The version SKILL.md declares. Absent means the skill predates the
    convention, which is itself a reason to re-install rather than to guess."""
    with open(os.path.join(SKILL, "SKILL.md"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
        head = f.read(4096)
    m = re.search(r"^version:\s*(\d+)\s*$", head, re.M)
    return int(m.group(1)) if m else 0


def whoami():
    """This process's real user NAME, resolved rather than read from $USER.

    ⚠️ NEVER PUT A LITERAL `$USER` IN A REMEDIATION. A message that has already
    had to fall back because $USER is unset must not then hand the operator
    `sudo chown $USER <path>` - under systemd-run, `su` without `-`, and
    `docker exec`, $USER is unset and that command fails with `the following
    required arguments were not provided: <FILE>`, i.e. the one line the blocked
    reader copy-pastes does not run. Resolve the name and interpolate it."""
    try:
        import pwd as _pwd
        return _pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a uid with no passwd entry is routine in a container
        return os.environ.get("USER") or os.environ.get("LOGNAME") or f"uid {os.getuid()}"


def errname(e):
    """A legible name for an OSError's errno.

    ⚠️ OSError.errno CAN BE None. TimeoutError, InterruptedError and the whole
    ConnectionError family are OSError subclasses that may carry no errno at all,
    and formatting one straight into `errno.errorcode.get(...)` renders
    `(None: None)` in the exact sentence the operator is going to act on."""
    if e.errno is None:
        return type(e).__name__
    return errno.errorcode.get(e.errno, str(e.errno))


def file_kind(mode):
    for pred, name in ((stat.S_ISDIR, "a directory"), (stat.S_ISFIFO, "a FIFO"),
                       (stat.S_ISSOCK, "a socket"), (stat.S_ISCHR, "a character device"),
                       (stat.S_ISBLK, "a block device")):
        if pred(mode):
            return name
    return "not a regular file"


def read_first_byte(path):
    """Open PATH and read one byte -> (ok, bytes_read, OSError or None).

    THE REAL OPERATION, not a question about the inode. See the header: this is
    what separates a file that is there from a file that can be USED, and every
    consumer of these paths reaches them by opening them.

    ⚠️ O_NONBLOCK AND THE S_ISREG TEST ARE WHAT STOP THIS FROM HANGING, AND
    NEITHER IS OPTIONAL. A plain `open(path, "rb")` on a FIFO BLOCKS FOREVER
    waiting for a writer - measured 2026-09-09 against a FIFO at token.path: a
    stat-based pair answers "empty" in milliseconds and a blocking open has to
    be killed at 124. A readiness check that never returns is the worst outcome
    available here: it is indistinguishable from a slow machine, nothing times
    it out, and a session that hangs with nobody at the terminal stays hung for
    hours. O_NONBLOCK makes the FIFO, socket and
    device cases return instead of blocking, and the fstat then reports the file
    TYPE as its own finding rather than letting a byte read decide.

    ⚠️ Reports the three outcomes SEPARATELY and refuses to collapse them:
    opened-and-had-content (True, 1, None), opened-and-was-EMPTY (True, 0, None),
    and never-opened-at-all (False, 0, the errno). Folding the last into "empty"
    or into a bare False is how a permission problem gets reported as a missing
    credential, which sends the operator to re-issue a token that is already
    correct. The caller writes a different remediation for each.

    Deliberately ONE byte and no parse: the requirement this check makes of these
    files is "present, non-empty, readable", and validating the CONTENT here
    would make the check stricter than the install it reports on."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | getattr(os, "O_NONBLOCK", 0))
    except OSError as e:
        return False, 0, e
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            err = OSError(errno.EINVAL, f"is {file_kind(st.st_mode)}, not a regular file")
            err.filename = path
            return False, 0, err
        return True, len(os.read(fd, 1)), None
    except OSError as e:
        return False, 0, e
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def wrong_shape(err):
    """Is ERR "this path is not a usable regular file" rather than a permission
    or a resource problem? The two need different remediations, and one line for
    both is the failure this whole file is about."""
    return err.errno in (errno.EISDIR, errno.ENOTDIR, errno.ELOOP,
                         errno.ENAMETOOLONG, errno.EINVAL)


def install_context():
    """The install's directory for this skill (CONTEXT.md, config.json,
    installation/.install-state.json), through fleetlib - or exit 2 naming why
    there is none."""
    sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(FLEET_ROOT, "lib"))
    try:
        from fleetlib import paths  # noqa: PLC0415
        return str(paths.skill_context(SKILL_NAME))
    except BaseException as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - fleetlib's refusals include SystemExit
        if isinstance(e, KeyboardInterrupt):
            raise
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no install context for {SKILL_NAME} ({type(e).__name__}: {e}). "
               f"Run from an install's checkout, or set FLEET_CONTEXT_ROOT to the install root.")


def claims_dir_problem(path):
    """None when PATH is fit to be the claims directory, else the line to print.
    See the header for why each property is required."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: no claims directory at {path} (claims_dir in host.json). "
                f"`fleet install --agents-root {os.path.dirname(path)}` creates it.")
    except OSError as e:
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: the claims directory {path} cannot be reached "
                f"({errname(e)}: {e.strerror or e}). `ls -ld` each component of it.")
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: the claims directory {path} is a symlink (to "
                f"{os.readlink(path)}); a link can be repointed under running agents, which "
                f"splits them into two claim spaces. Make it a real directory: move any claims "
                f"out of the target, `rm {path}`, then `fleet install --agents-root {os.path.dirname(path)}`.")
    if not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        # file_kind() names only the NON-regular types (it serves the token leg),
        # so a regular file has to be named here or it reads "not a regular file".
        kind = "a regular file" if stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) else file_kind(st.st_mode)
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: {path} is {kind}, not the claims directory. "
                f"Remove it, then `fleet install --agents-root {os.path.dirname(path)}`.")
    if st.st_uid != os.getuid():
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: the claims directory {path} is owned by uid {st.st_uid}, not by "
                f"{whoami()}, the user every agent runs as. A machine ADMIN repairs it: "
                f"`sudo chown {whoami()} {path} && chmod 700 {path}`.")
    mode = stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode)
    if mode != 0o700:
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: the claims directory {path} is mode {mode:o}, not 700, so "
                f"another account could claim every request. Run: `chmod 700 {path}`.")
    base = os.path.join(path, f".install-check.{os.getpid()}.{secrets.token_hex(4)}")
    tmp, linked = base + ".tmp", base + ".link"
    try:
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
        try:
            os.write(fd, b"x")
            os.fsync(fd)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        os.link(tmp, linked)
        try:
            os.link(tmp, linked)
        except FileExistsError:
            pass
        else:
            return (f"NOT INSTALLED: link(2) in {path} replaced an existing name instead of "
                    f"refusing it, so claim.py cannot guarantee one winner on this filesystem. "
                    f"Put claims_dir on a local POSIX filesystem.")
    except OSError as e:
        return (f"NOT INSTALLED: the claims directory {path} cannot do what claim.py does there "
                f"({errname(e)}: {e.strerror or e} on create/fsync/link). Check the mount "
                f"(`findmnt -T {path}`): it must be a writable local filesystem with hard links.")
    finally:
        for p in (tmp, linked):
            try:
                os.unlink(p)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                pass
    return None


def main() -> None:
    want = declared_version()
    context = install_context()
    state_file = os.path.join(context, "installation", ".install-state.json")

    # Opened, not stat'ed. An unreadable or directory-shaped config.json would
    # otherwise pass this leg and fail inside `import watch` below, which reports
    # it as "watch.py does not load" - a true sentence about the wrong file, and
    # the reader then goes looking for a bug in the watcher.
    cfg = os.path.join(context, "config.json")
    ok, _, err = read_first_byte(cfg)
    if not ok:
        if err.errno == errno.ENOENT:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no config.json at {cfg}. Run: `install -m 644 "
                   f"{os.path.join(SKILL, 'config.example.json')} {cfg}`, then fill it in.")
        if wrong_shape(err):
            # ⚠️ "SOME COMPONENT OF", not "this file". ENOTDIR and ELOOP are
            # raised just as readily by a PARENT component, and a remediation
            # that assumes the final one hands over `rm -rf <full path> &&
            # install ...` whose second half then fails with "No such file or
            # directory" - measured. Naming the path and letting the operator
            # find the offending component is less satisfying and correct.
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: some component of {cfg} is not a readable regular file "
                   f"({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}). `ls -ld` each component to "
                   f"find it, remove what is in the way, and put the template there: "
                   f"`install -m 644 {os.path.join(SKILL, 'config.example.json')} {cfg}`, then fill it in.")
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: config.json is present at {cfg} but cannot be read "
               f"({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}). Run: "
               f"`sudo chown {whoami()} {cfg} && chmod 0644 {cfg}`.")

    # Import the watcher itself. Its module scope loads the config, resolves
    # every required key and resolves the token path; a SystemExit here is the
    # watcher's own WATCH-CONFIG diagnostic, which is better than anything this
    # script could word. SLACK_WATCH_CONFIG is SET to the install's config, so the
    # check validates the install and not whichever agent happens to call it.
    sys.path.insert(0, SKILL)
    os.environ["SLACK_WATCH_CONFIG"] = cfg
    try:
        import watch  # noqa: E402
    except SystemExit as e:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {e}")
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - any import failure is "not installed"
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: watch.py does not load ({type(e).__name__}: {e})")

    # THE TOKEN, OPENED. Every Slack call in this skill starts by reading this
    # file, so reading it is the only test that measures what the watcher needs.
    # The three outcomes are kept apart on purpose (see read_first_byte): absent,
    # unreadable and empty have three different fixes, and the middle one is the
    # state an exists()+getsize() pair reports as installed.
    ok, nbytes, err = read_first_byte(watch.TOKEN_FILE)
    if not ok:
        if err.errno == errno.ENOENT:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no bot token file at {watch.TOKEN_FILE}. Write the "
                   f"bot token there: `install -m 0600 /dev/null {watch.TOKEN_FILE}` then "
                   f"paste the bot token into it.")
        # ⚠️ ONE REMEDIATION PER CAUSE. A permission errno and a wrong-shape
        # errno reach this branch together, and `chmod` is actively wrong for the
        # second: told to chmod a DIRECTORY the operator runs a command that
        # succeeds and changes nothing, then reports the check broken. Do not
        # flatten these two back into one line.
        if wrong_shape(err):
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: token.path in config.json points at {watch.TOKEN_FILE}, "
                   f"and some component of that path is not a readable regular file "
                   f"({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}). `ls -ld` each component to find "
                   f"it, remove what is in the way, and write the token as a plain file: "
                   f"`install -m 0600 /dev/null {watch.TOKEN_FILE}`, then paste the bot "
                   f"token into it.")
        if err.errno == errno.EROFS:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: the bot token file at {watch.TOKEN_FILE} is on a "
                   f"READ-ONLY filesystem ({err.strerror or err}); chown and chmod both "
                   f"return EROFS there, so neither is the fix. A machine ADMIN remounts it "
                   f"read-write, or moves the token store: check `mount | grep \" $(df "
                   f"--output=target {watch.TOKEN_FILE} | tail -1) \"`.")
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: the bot token file at {watch.TOKEN_FILE} exists but cannot "
               f"be read by {whoami()} ({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}), so every "
               f"Slack call this skill makes will fail at its first line. A machine ADMIN "
               f"repairs it: `sudo chown {whoami()} {watch.TOKEN_FILE} && "
               f"chmod 0600 {watch.TOKEN_FILE}`.")
    if nbytes == 0:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: bot token file is empty at {watch.TOKEN_FILE}")

    # THE CLAIMS DIRECTORY, resolved by claim.py's own resolver (see the header).
    claim_py = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(SKILL), "chat-transport", "claim.py")
    try:
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("fleet_claim", claim_py)
        claim = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        spec.loader.exec_module(claim)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - any load failure is "not installed"
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: claim.py does not load from {claim_py} ({type(e).__name__}: {e})")
    try:
        claims_dir = claim.configured_dir()
    except claim.Fault as e:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {e}")
    problem = claims_dir_problem(claims_dir)
    if problem:
        out(2, problem)

    # Artifacts are all present. Only the recorded version can still be wrong.
    # Opened rather than exists()-then-opened: one operation, and its errno is
    # what distinguishes "never installed here" from "installed and unreadable".
    try:
        with open(state_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            state = json.load(f)
        have = int(state.get("version", -1))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        out(1, f"STALE: installed, but no {state_file} records which version "
               f"(SKILL.md declares {want}). Re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        out(1, f"STALE: .install-state.json at {state_file} unreadable "
               f"({type(e).__name__}: {e}). Run: `chmod 0644 {state_file}`, or delete it and "
               f"re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md to rewrite it.")

    host = state.get("host")
    if host and host != socket.gethostname():
        out(1, f"STALE: last installed on {host}, not on this machine "
               f"({socket.gethostname()}). The state file travels with the repo; "
               f"the install does not. Re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")

    if have < want:
        out(1, f"STALE: installed version {have}, SKILL.md declares {want}. "
               f"Re-run installation/INSTRUCTION.md.")
    if have > want:
        out(1, f"STALE: installed version {have} is NEWER than SKILL.md's {want}. "
               f"The fleet checkout is older than the install; upgrade the fleet "
               f"(`fleet upgrade`) before running.")

    # ⚠️ THIS SPEAKS ONLY FOR THE INSTALL'S config.json. The check sets
    # SLACK_WATCH_CONFIG to it on purpose (so it validates the install, not
    # whichever agent happens to be calling it), which means the per-agent configs
    # the LIVE watchers actually run from are never read here. A green from this script
    # says nothing about them; the only thing that validates one of those is
    # arming the watcher against it. The line below says so, so that a reader
    # cannot take this pass for a statement about a running watcher.
    out(0, f"OK: installed and current (version {want}), "
           f"workspace {watch.WORKSPACE_DOMAIN}, owner {watch.OWNER_CONFIG_DIR}, "
           f"claims dir {claims_dir} (link probe ok) "
           f"(of the install's config.json only; the live per-agent configs are not read here)")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
